UX Microcopy — Rewriting My Own To-Do List App
Project: Self-directed practice (revisiting a 2023 project)
Goal: Show how the same interface reads differently once the copy does actual work
Tone: Plain, specific, human

I built a simple to-do list app in 2023 as a beginner project. Revisiting the interface copy became a useful before/after: same features, same layout — just labels that assume nothing about the user versus labels that talk to them.

## Before (2023, beginner labels)

- "Task 1"
- "Save" / "Delete"
- "No tasks."

## After (today)

- "Cook dinner — 7:00 PM"
- "Add to today" / "Done, remove it"
- "Nothing on your list yet. Add your first task."

## Why These Work

- **Specific beats generic** — "Task 1" tells the user nothing; "Cook dinner — 7:00 PM" shows what a real entry looks like and models the format
- **Verbs describe outcomes, not actions** — "Done, remove it" tells the user what happens, not just what button they're pressing
- **Empty states do work** — "No tasks." is a dead end; "Nothing on your list yet. Add your first task." is an instruction
- **No jargon, no cleverness** — the goal is a user who never has to guess what a button does

Same interface, same code — the only thing that changed is whether the copy treats the user like they need to be told what to do.
